{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1456184-mage-20-and-soaking-magickal-damage-question","id":1456184,"title":"Mage 20 and Soaking Magickal Damage Question:","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-08-05T07:36:25-07:00","id":1456184,"title":"Mage 20 and Soaking Magickal Damage Question:","content":"So under the soaking magickal damage section of M20 it says: <br /> <br /> &quot;Magickal attacks that unleash physical force \u2013 blades, bolts, storms, etc. \u2013 can be soaked like any other physical damage. The usual rules, presented in Chapter Nine, apply when soaking damage.&quot;<br /> <br /> and<br /> <br /> &quot;All-out Reality-fucking, however, is hard to endure. Vulgar attacks of pure reality-alteration power (curses, transmutations, possession, etc.) cannot be soaked unless the target has countermagick or some other specific protection against the Effect in question.&quot;<br /> <br /> So here's a query, if a mage lets say used entropy (or life) as a pattern attack, and cast it in the form of a vulgar curse, would it be soakable? Your pattern is innately physical, therefore it could count as physical damage, however it would be cast in the form of a curse, &quot;Avada Kedavra!&quot; or something along those lines. How would you rule this?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26413-thatonekiasyd","name":"ThatOneKiasyd","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26413_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"35"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-05T09:20:10-07:00","id":1456189,"title":"","content":"I'd say that it is option 2, a magical attack. Unless something is produced - beyond the magic - to produce the attack ie a fire ball.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26145-babi_gog","name":"babi_gog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26145_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-05T09:41:57-07:00","id":1456191,"title":"","content":"As a basic rule, I see Forces 3 attacks as being soakable, which is offset by the additional damage that all Forces-based attacks inflict. Damage from any other sphere is generally not soakable. Any sphere effect, damaging or not, can be countered by a mage/technocrat with the countermagick/anti-magick rules.<br /> <br /> The only thing I can think of that I'm not 100% sure how I'd rule it is a Prime 3 attack (with no other spheres) - I can make an argument for this being soakable, and also make an argument for it to be unsoakable.<br /> <br /> As for exactly HOW it's enacted, it's irrelevant. An Entropy 4 direct attack cannot be soaked whether it was done by a Traditionalist via some sort of curse or a Technocrat using an Entropy Acceleration Projector.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26904,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","user":"breggie","date":". 08-06-2021, 09:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","name":"breggie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26904_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-06T09:08:46-07:00","id":1456247,"title":"","content":"It's not so much that Vulgar attacks tend to be unsoakable, but that most Aggravated (and thus unsoakable) damage tends to be Vulgar. Pattern damage certainly is, as is transmutation damage. There's nothing about going Vulgar that makes damage unsoakable if it wasn't already, though.<br /> <br /> So your Entropic curse does Aggravated damage, and Mages can't soak that without help.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-08T04:56:15-07:00","id":1456344,"title":"","content":"Yes. It all depends on what the damage is going to be - for example, basic Mind blasts count as bashing damage, so can be soaked. Fire? Nope. A storm of magical cuts? Nope, those are lethal. Lightning? Nope, lethal. Blunt kinetic force caused by magic (an invisible fist, being flung against a wall etc.)? Yep, that's bashing, etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-08T07:49:15-07:00","id":1456350,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1456184-mage-20-and-soaking-magickal-damage-question?p=1456344#post1456344\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes. It all depends on what the damage is going to be - for example, basic Mind blasts count as bashing damage, so can be soaked. Fire? Nope. A storm of magical cuts? Nope, those are lethal. Lightning? Nope, lethal. Blunt kinetic force caused by magic (an invisible fist, being flung against a wall etc.)? Yep, that's bashing, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>See, I understand this a different way. Even a Mind 3 attack that inflicts bashing damage should not be soakable except by a mage that has at least one dot in the Mind sphere. And that\u2019s via countermagick, not soaking with stamina.<br /> <br /> I read it as \u201cdirect magickal attacks\u201d are not soakable. Forces is not direct - you create and direct a force (kinetic energy, heat, electricity) and that force causes damage. With other spheres, the magick itself is ripping apart the victim\u2019s pattern on a metaphysical level and regardless of damage type this cannot be soaked without sphere magick. <br /> <br /> I\u2019m not able to reference specific rules at the moment but maybe I can find some examples later.<br /> <br /> And for reference my current character has no trouble soaking lethal damage with a constantly maintained Forces 2 energy dampening effect. With Prime 3 or Life 3 added, he can also soak aggravated damage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26904,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","user":"breggie","date":". 08-08-2021, 10:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","name":"breggie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26904_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-09T04:44:22-07:00","id":1456414,"title":"","content":"I think this comes down to what the mental attack is doing. In a &quot;classic&quot; psychic battle, the characters are hurling mental energy at each other. Or attacking with an onslaught of emotions. Or trying to overwhelm the senses of the other person. Those are attacks that seem soakable to me, especially the second two. They also don't come across as direct, any more than throwing a fireball at someone is direct.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-09T06:00:28-07:00","id":1456419,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1456184-mage-20-and-soaking-magickal-damage-question?p=1456414#post1456414\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think this comes down to what the mental attack is doing. In a &quot;classic&quot; psychic battle, the characters are hurling mental energy at each other. Or attacking with an onslaught of emotions. Or trying to overwhelm the senses of the other person. Those are attacks that seem soakable to me, especially the second two. They also don't come across as direct, any more than throwing a fireball at someone is direct.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thinking about this I agree, maybe Mind is a bad example. I would argue that by RAW, Entropy and Life direct attacks should not be soakable even by a mage that can soak aggravated damage. Of course it\u2019s probably a minor and moot distinction. In all likelihood such attacks will inflict enough damage that a few soak dice won\u2019t make a ton of difference.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","name":"breggie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26904_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-09T10:33:10-07:00","id":1456439,"title":"","content":"I disagree. There's nothing about Life and Entropy that makes their pattern damage any different from any other pattern damage. So if someone can soak pattern damage (aka Aggravated damage) they can soak those.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-09T16:44:40-07:00","id":1456471,"title":"","content":"It's.. honestly kinda vaguely worded especially if you consider the kinds of damage in 406 so I think its going to really depend on how you interpret it and you won't get a clear cut answer. I'd say treat it as conditional based on: <br /> <br /> - How the damage is caused and how it interacts with the target. This is perhaps the messiest. In broad terms damage involving macroscopic physical force/impact against a corporeal form is probably 'stamina' based soak (contrasted with other types described in the rulebook) Punches, kicks, knife stabs, bullets, etc. I think there are lots of ways you can inflict 'physical' trauma via various spheres (as is described in the 'Thunder Punch' section) but the effects/impact could vary greatly. <br /> <br /> I would also say that the other conditons for soaking a given kind of damage with stamina apply. Humans normally can only soak bashing damage and need either specific rules or gimmicks to allow them to soak lethal (or even aggravated, assuming such a distinction is drawn for mortals which is not always the case.) Mages are still basically human and need specific uses of magic to soak lethal and aggravated so those conditions would still have to be met. <br /> <br /> Complicating things is that 'aggravated' damage is depicted as incorporating all kinds of things like fire, acid, radiation, really bad poisons (not necessarily diseases though but I could see a case being made there), explosions which are all explicitly 'physical' atrocities. Although I'm not sure that explanation works well in a broader context (sunlight causing aggravated damage to vampires is more of a 'curse' than physical 'impact' or because the sunlight causes thermal burns arguably. Otherwise you could probably argue a specific combination of EM radiation simulating sunlight could also cause aggravated damage. As I recall this is verboten logic in even Mage.) Also looking more I just realized that if you spend a point of quintessence you can make even mind-sphere attacks aggravated just like with 'physical' attacks. Also forces is described as being 'aggravated' if it inflicts fire/electrical damage. I could also see 'matter' based attacks causing aggravated if it were acid say (not exactly something that suits 'forces' normally I think.) Certain attacks (radiation, electricity, poisons/disease) would also act on scales smallre than the eye can see (smaller than a knife punch or bullet) hence the macroscopic distinction. <br /> <br /> - You also have 'psychic' damage that in game terms is bashing and soaked by willpower that represents a form of 'intangible' damage to the 'consciousness or soul' (although like 'aggravated' this seems to be something more geared towards the 'awakened' rather than mortals given the distinctions made) - attacks by the mind sphere are the obvious one in mage terms but they also mention torture and shocks causing <a href=\"https://www.britannica.com/science/psychosomatic-disorder\" target=\"_blank\">'psychosomatic'</a> pain which brings us to one of those aforementioned murky areas. Like the bashing damage might reflect purely intangible effects on the 'vitality' of the person (all nebulous) or maybe it could be mental anguish/stress being so extreme that it adversely affects the physical body and causes tangible damage (which means you could 'technically' argue it is soaked physically. Psychosomatic kinda trips it up for me in that respect, at least.) <br /> <br /> - then you have what might be considered the 'everything else' category in terms of damage which includes the aforementioned 'reality fucking' which seems to be a gamut of things which may not be directly damage causing. Tenatively given what is described (reality-alteration, transmutation, possession, curses) you MIGHT assume this is something that would not be aggravated damage but still very magical/supernatural in nature (meaning its not a macroscopic physical blow or psychic trauma) in which case its 'soaked' with countermagic or whatever appropriate defensive measure the target may possess against that attack. This is an obviously bigger gray area obviously given it can overlap with aggravated damage or how you define 'reality fucking' in terms of effects. Like reality fucking could still cause psychic or physical trauma in ways (it may just be indirect damage rather than direct) so you could still see a stamina or willpower soak. Furthermore in many cases the damage avoidance via countermagick feels like its better applied as 'defense' rather than 'soak' (like parrying or dodging basically) - the exceptions being where the effects on the pattern transcend sheer physical resilience in terms of altering it (Entropy-based disintegration, Prime-based attacks, etc.) *might* trigger a countermagic 'soak'... but then again if its aggravated you could argue its better handled by physical or psychic trauma. <br /> <br /> Ultimately I think it will be situational.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-11T10:38:31-07:00","id":1456610,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mister_Dunpeal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1456184-mage-20-and-soaking-magickal-damage-question?p=1456471#post1456471\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...<br /> Complicating things is that 'aggravated' damage is depicted as incorporating all kinds of things like fire, acid, radiation, really bad poisons (not necessarily diseases though but I could see a case being made there), explosions which are all explicitly 'physical' atrocities...</div> </div> </div> </div>I am not sure that the books (mage in particular) treat these as aggravated. They very well may and I'm not going to look at the moment, but if so I would argue that is wrong. Fire/Sunlight are aggravated damage for vampires, but I would argue not for mages. I would rule that fire, acid, radiation, poison, and explosions would all be lethal damage for mages, just like they are for humans, werewolves (WtA), imbued hunters (HtR), and demons (DtF). Sunlight can hurt mages too, just like for humans - have you ever had a severe sunburn? Of course it takes a long time so it can mostly be ignored as a source of damage.<br /> <br /> I would rule that for mages, only magickal and supernatural damage is aggravated (Entropy 4, Life 3, and Prime 3, as well as werewolf claws or vampire fangs.). Forces 3 attacks would inflict either bashing or lethal, depending on the force (i.e. kinetic or electricity). Mind 3 would inflict bashing, perhaps soakable with willpower but only if you want to introduce that special rule, otherwise Stamina is fine. I would deny (house rule) the use of quintessence to make Forces or Mind attacks inflict aggravated, though of course using Forces 3 to attack a vampire with fire would be aggravated, not because it's Forces, but because fire inflicts aggravated on vampires.<br /> <br /> Through this conversation I think I may have changed my mind on the direct magickal damage thing - I now conclude that simply handling all soaking for mages by using the damage types is simple and appropriate. Many mages have ways to soak lethal or aggravated damage, and they can always use countermagick. A soak really isn't that significant of protection anyway (just see the discussions about Fortitude over in the V20 forum section), so allowing it for a mage that uses the appropriate effect is not really that big of an advantage anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","name":"breggie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26904_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-11T12:11:38-07:00","id":1456613,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>breggie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1456184-mage-20-and-soaking-magickal-damage-question?p=1456610#post1456610\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I am not sure that the books (mage in particular) treat these as aggravated. They very well may and I'm not going to look at the moment, but if so I would argue that is wrong. Fire/Sunlight are aggravated damage for vampires, but I would argue not for mages. I would rule that fire, acid, radiation, poison, and explosions would all be lethal damage for mages, just like they are for humans, werewolves (WtA), imbued hunters (HtR), and demons (DtF). Sunlight can hurt mages too, just like for humans - have you ever had a severe sunburn? Of course it takes a long time so it can mostly be ignored as a source of damage.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fire, acid, intense radiation, virulent toxins, and explosions are specifically called out as sources of Aggravated damage by M20. I'm pretty sure Werewolves take Aggravated damage from fire too (though I think they can soak it). Almost everything takes Aggravated damage from Fire, really.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that insofar as Mages and mortals are concerned, there isn't a lot of difference between Lethal and Aggravated. A human who takes 5 lethal damage is as close to death as a human who takes 5 Aggravated damage. For Mage purposes the only real difference is in the cost and vulgarity of causing, healing, and defending against it.<br /> <br /> Now personally, I don't like that the vulgarity is linked to something other than the Consensus, but the cost part makes sense to me. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I would rule that for mages, only magickal and supernatural damage is aggravated (Entropy 4, Life 3, and Prime 3, as well as werewolf claws or vampire fangs.). Forces 3 attacks would inflict either bashing or lethal, depending on the force (i.e. kinetic or electricity). Mind 3 would inflict bashing, perhaps soakable with willpower but only if you want to introduce that special rule, otherwise Stamina is fine. I would deny (house rule) the use of quintessence to make Forces or Mind attacks inflict aggravated, though of course using Forces 3 to attack a vampire with fire would be aggravated, not because it's Forces, but because fire inflicts aggravated on vampires. </div> </div> </div>That's pretty much RAW. Entropy, Life, and Prime have natural access to aggravated damage. Adding Prime 2 and a point of quint can elevate the others to aggravated. Otherwise mind is naturally bashing, and the rest depend on the attack and the target.. Attack with a force or substance that does aggravated damage to the target and you do aggravated damage. Attack with a force they can soak and they can soak it. Etc.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}